  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 255 AM EDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nThursday and Friday:\n\nThe end of the work week will bring mainly dry conditions and near-\nseasonal/slightly-above-seasonal temperatures, thanks to a mid-\n/upper-level pattern that favors subsidence and high pressure across \nthe Southeast. \n\nSaturday through Monday:\n\nOur eyes will be on the Gulf over the coming days regarding the \ndevelopment and evolution of an area of low pressure currently in \nthe southwestern Gulf. At this time, the National Hurricane Center \n(NHC) has an 80% chance of tropical formation in the next 7 days. \nFeatures that will impact the track of this system include: high \npressure along the Eastern Seaboard (which could induce cold air \ndamming or the \"wedge\" across the forecast area) and a longwave \ntrough across the western CONUS. Steering flow between these two \nfeatures should bring whatever comes of the system northward into \nthe northern Gulf and somewhere along the Gulf Coast region. As far \nas potential impacts to north and central Georgia -- it is much too \nearly to say. Models struggle to handle systems before a defined \ncirculation develops, which is the stage we are at now.\n\nA couple variables that are currently in our forecast over the \nweekend into early next week -- breezy conditions and the return of \nshowers and storms for at least portions of the forecast area. How \nbreezy it is will largely be determined by the interplay of the \nwedge and the proximity of the potentially-tropical system. At this \ntime, sustained winds up to 15 mph and gusts up to 25-30 mph are on \nthe table. As far as rainfall goes, should tropical moisture \nadvection set up over the forecast area, periods of heavy rain and \nresulting flash flooding and/or rises on rivers, creeks, and streams \nwould be possibilities.\n\nThe weekend and Monday should bring cooler daytime high temperatures \n-- in the 70s to lower 80s.",
